    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a hypertrophy program for a client and wanted to double check some physiology stuff. Can you explain how leucine specifically interacts with the mTOR pathway to trigger protein synthesis? Also, how much of a caloric surplus do we actually need to see a positive shift in nitrogen balance? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine activates the mTOR pathway by binding to the mTOR complex and preventing its inactivation by FKBP12. This results in an increase in the phosphorylation of downstream targets, such as S6K1 and 4E-BP1, promoting protein synthesis and cell growth. The presence of sufficient amino acids, especially leucine, is essential for initiating this signaling cascade.\n\nRegarding caloric surplus, a minimum of 1.2-1.4 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight (or 0.5-0.6 grams per pound) is required to achieve a positive nitrogen balance, indicating that the body has sufficient dietary nitrogen to support muscle growth. This level of protein intake is generally associated with a 0.5-1% increase in body weight and muscle mass per week. A caloric surplus that exceeds 500-750 calories above maintenance levels accelerates muscle protein synthesis.",
      "source": "target",
